/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The Clausius inequality says ∮δQ/T ≤ 0 for any cycle; the value is zero for a reversible cycle.
   - δQrev is the heat transfer in a reversible process, in kJ; T is absolute temperature, in K; dS is the entropy change, in kJ/K.
   - S is extensive: 2 kg has twice the entropy of 1 kg at the same state.
   - On a temperature-entropy diagram, plot T in K against s in kJ/kg·K.
   - An isothermal reversible process is a horizontal line.
   - Slopes.
   - For an ideal gas with constant specific heats, integrate ds = cv dT/T + R dv/v and ds = cp dT/T − R dp/p.
   - m is the mass in kg; cv is the specific heat at constant volume in kJ/kg·K; T1, T2 are absolute temperatures in K; R is the gas constant in kJ/kg·K; v1, v2 are specific volumes in m³/kg; ΔS is in kJ/K.
   - cp is the specific heat at constant pressure in kJ/kg·K; p1, p2 are pressures in the same unit, usually kPa; the other symbols are as above.
   - Use cp when pressure is held constant and cv when volume is held constant.
*/
