/* book Worked example: Q: 2 kg of air is heated at constant pressure from 300 K to 600 K. Take cp = 1.005 kJ/kg·K, cv = 0.718 kJ/kg·K and R = 0.287 kJ/kg·K. Find the entropy change of the air. Check the result by treating the same change as constant-volume heating followed by isothermal expansion.
   1. List the data: m = 2 kg, T₁ = 300 K, T₂ = 600 K, cp = 1.005 kJ/kg·K, cv = 0.718 kJ/kg·K, R = 0.287 kJ/kg·K.
   2. For constant pressure, use ΔS = m cp ln(T₂/T₁).
   3. T₂/T₁ = 600/300 = 2; ln 2 = 0.6931.
   4. ΔS = 2 × 1.005 × 0.6931 = 1.393 kJ/K.
   5. Check. Constant-pressure heating from 300 K to 600 K also doubles the specific volume, so v₂/v₁ = 2.
   6. Constant-volume part: m cv ln(T₂/T₁) = 2 × 0.718 × 0.6931 = 0.995 kJ/K.
   7. Isothermal part: m R ln(v₂/v₁) = 2 × 0.287 × 0.6931 = 0.398 kJ/K.
   8. Sum = 0.995 + 0.398 = 1.393 kJ/K, the same value.
   Answer: The entropy change is 1.393 kJ/K, positive because heat is added. */
