/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - For any process between state 1 and state 2, reversible or not, the entropy account reads as follows.
   - ΔSsystem + ΔSsurroundings = Sgen ≥ 0 ΔSsystem is the entropy change of the system in kJ/K, equal to S₂ − S₁; ΔSsurroundings is the entropy change of everything outside the system, in kJ/K; Sgen is the entropy generated during the process, i
   - The equality holds only for a fully reversible process.
   - Seal the system and its surroundings into one box and you have an isolated system.
   - Compare this with the first law.
   - Sgen is a quantitative measure of irreversibility.
   - Heat through a finite temperature difference is the commonest.
   - Sgen = Q (1/Tcold − 1/Thot) Q is the heat in kJ passing from the hot body to the cold body; Tcold and Thot are in K; Sgen is in kJ/K.
   - A reversible adiabatic process has Q = 0 and no friction or mixing, so Sgen = 0 and ΔS = 0.
   - The same inequality answers a different question: will this change happen on its own?
*/
