/* book exam traps: ΔS of the system alone may be negative — freezing water and compressing gas both do it. Only system plus surroundings is guaranteed non-negative, so write the full account before you conclude anything. | Sgen is in kJ/K, not kJ. Dividing Q by a temperature in °C gives nonsense; convert to kelvin first. | A reversible adiabatic process is isentropic (ΔS = 0). A reversible isothermal process is not: ΔS = Q/T is finite. Do not label every reversible process isentropic. | When the vessel is not insulated, the surroundings' entropy change must be added with its sign. Many answers lose marks by silently dropping it. | A positive ΔS says the process can happen, not that it happens quickly. Direction comes from thermodynamics; rate comes from kinetics. */
